{"seq": 1, "type": "user_message", "ts": "2026-11-22T19:24:15.142429+00:00", "payload": {"text": "# Your workspace\n\nYou are in `/work`, inside a Linux container with no network access. Write a Go program here.\n[Maelstrom](https://github.com/jepsen-io/maelstrom) will start that program once for every node\nof a simulated cluster, send it client requests, and check that the answers are correct.\n\nWhat is installed:\n\n- Go 1.27.\n- Maelstrom 0.2.4, as the `maelstrom` command, so you can test your program yourself.\n- Maelstrom's Go library, already in the module cache. Start your module with:\n\n      go mod init node\n      go get github.com/jepsen-io/maelstrom/demo/go@v0.0.0-20260710121306-480a8197026d\n\nA node talks to the rest of the cluster through standard input and output: it reads one JSON\nmessage per line on stdin and writes one per line on stdout. Each message has a `src`, a `dest`\nand a `body`, and the body's `type` says what it is. Anything written to stderr is a log line.\nThe library does that framing for you: `maelstrom.NewNode()`, then `Handle` a message type,\n`Reply` to a request, `Send` or `RPC` to another node, and `Run` to start. It also answers the\n`init` message that tells each node its own ID and the IDs of all the nodes.\n\n# Read-committed transactions that stay available\n\nWrite a node for a cluster of two that together keep a store of integer keys and values, and run\ntransactions against it. Clients send each node one kind of request.\n\n`txn` carries a list of operations, each a read (`r`) or a write (`w`) of one key. A read is sent\nwith a null value; the reply carries the same list with each read's value filled in, or null for\na key with nothing written yet. Writes come back as they were sent:\n\n    {\"type\": \"txn\", \"msg_id\": 1, \"txn\": [[\"r\", 1, null], [\"w\", 1, 6], [\"w\", 2, 9], [\"r\", 2, null]]}\n    {\"type\": \"txn_ok\", \"in_reply_to\": 1, \"txn\": [[\"r\", 1, 3], [\"w\", 1, 6], [\"w\", 2, 9], [\"r\", 2, 9]]}\n\nThe store must be read committed:\n\n- A read never sees a value that the transaction writing it overwrote before it finished. If a\n  transaction writes 6 and then 7 to a key, other transactions may see 7 but never 6.\n- A read never sees a write from a transaction that was answered with an error.\n\nIt must also be totally available: every transaction must be answered with `txn_ok`. During the\ntest the network between the two nodes is split and healed again and again, and a message sent\nacross a split is lost without any error. Clients can still reach both nodes, so a node can't\nwait on the other one before answering. Writes made on one node should reach the other once the\nnetwork allows.\n\n## How it's graded\n\nYou have 60 minutes and 200 steps, a step being one reply from you, however many tools it runs. If either runs out, you are stopped where you are.\n\nWhen you stop, your program is built with `go build -o node .` and tested 3 times with:\n\n    maelstrom test --workload txn-rw-register --bin ./node --availability total --concurrency 2n --consistency-models read-committed --nemesis partition --node-count 2 --rate 1000 --time-limit 20\n\nThe runs are independent, each with its own timing and faults. The attempt passes only if it builds and every run is valid.\n"}}
